<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 658</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samo was a Frankish merchant who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a>, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> in a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> around 623 and was then chosen as their king, ruling for some thirty-five years. In about 631 he defeated the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a> at Wogastisburg. His realm, probably centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, is the first known Slavic polity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samo.jpg" alt="Samo" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Palickap · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%A1klo_(vrch),_S%C3%A1mo_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Almost everything known of Samo comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a>, written about 660, the only contemporary source; later accounts all derive from it. Fredegar calls him a Frank by birth from the pago Senonago, which could be Soignies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. A text written at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> in the ninth century, the Conversio Bagoariorum et Carantanorum, makes him a Carantanian merchant instead, and Slavic nationalist accounts have often preferred a Slavic origin. According to Fredegar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> had long been subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, who wintered among them, took their wives and daughters and demanded levies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Samo went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> as a merchant, perhaps one of those who supplied them with arms for their frequent revolts. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, he accompanied their army, and a great many Avars fell to Wendish swords. His victories led to his election as king. The date is uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a> places his arrival in the fortieth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Chlothar II</a>, 623 to 624, but many scholars think the rising followed the Avar defeat at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 626. Samo secured his throne by marrying into the major Wendish families, with at least twelve wives, twenty-two sons and fifteen daughters.</p><p>His realm probably lay in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, and is generally thought to have included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>. Whether it also took in Carantania is disputed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> invaded in 630 over banditry against Frankish merchants, Samo's forces routed the army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a> near Wogastisburg in 631, and the Franks lost most of the besieging armies. Samo then raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and the Sorbian duke Dervan, until then subject to the Franks, joined him. In 641 the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radulf-of-thuringia">Radulf of Thuringia</a> sought an alliance with Samo against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-iii">Sigebert III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Samo ruled, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a>'s count, for thirty-five years, and died in 658 or 659. His title did not pass to his sons, and little is known of his union afterwards, though it is generally assumed to have ended. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> returned to their earlier lands, and the Moravian and Nitravian principalities of the late 8th century are the first firm trace of these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> is viewed as a continuation or successor of his realm, which is often called the first Slavic state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolt of <b>Samo's Slavs</b> around 623 and the rise of Kubrat's Great Bulgaria in the 630s were both reactions against Avar lordship, and so were linked to the formation of new states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Samo's</b> death around 658 they formed an independent principality centred on the Zollfeld plain near Karnburg in modern Carinthia.</blockquote><a href="/state/carantania">Carantania</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Samo</b>, a Frankish merchant trading with them, fought so well that they chose him as king.</blockquote><a href="/state/samos-empire">Samo's Empire</a></li></ul></section>
